\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Fiducial \textsc  {bind}{} lightcone maps over the full $5\times 5\,{\rm  deg}^2$ field of view. \textit  {Left:} \textsc  {bind}{} convergence $\kappa $ at source redshift $z_{\rm  s}=1.0$. \textit  {Middle:} the paired DMO-only convergence at identical geometry, used as the common-mode reference for all suppression ratios in this paper. \textit  {Right:} the \textsc  {bind}{} Compton-$y$ map from the same realization. The painted field is visually distinct from the DMO progenitor as an overall smoothing of the field-wide small-scale texture (most apparent in the diffuse, non-halo regions) rather than a localized change at individual peaks, and the $y$ map traces halo cores with compact, feedback-sculpted morphology --- the qualitative signature the quantitative validation in Figure~\ref {fig:validation_field} confirms statistically.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:map_gallery}{{1}{5}{Fiducial \bind {} lightcone maps over the full $5\times 5\,{\rm deg}^2$ field of view. \textit {Left:} \bind {} convergence $\kappa $ at source redshift $\zs =1.0$. \textit {Middle:} the paired DMO-only convergence at identical geometry, used as the common-mode reference for all suppression ratios in this paper. \textit {Right:} the \bind {} Compton-$y$ map from the same realization. The painted field is visually distinct from the DMO progenitor as an overall smoothing of the field-wide small-scale texture (most apparent in the diffuse, non-halo regions) rather than a localized change at individual peaks, and the $y$ map traces halo cores with compact, feedback-sculpted morphology --- the qualitative signature the quantitative validation in Figure~\ref {fig:validation_field} confirms statistically}{figure.1}{}}
